@page "/user-roles"

<PageTitle>User Roles - OperationsFlow</PageTitle>

<div class="operations-dashboard user-roles-page">
    <PageIntro Eyebrow="Production Planning"
               Title="User Roles"
               Subtitle="A production-focused overview of the user roles, permission boundaries, access levels, and approval responsibilities OperationsFlow would need before becoming a live business system.">
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="technical-overview">Technical Overview</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="implementation-plan">Implementation Plan</a>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="prototype-scope">Prototype Scope</a>
    </PageIntro>

    <div class="page-purpose-note">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> use this page to explain the production permissions model. The current prototype does not include real authentication or role-based access, but this page shows how admins, managers, reviewers, owners, standard users, and read-only viewers would be separated in a production version.
    </div>

    <section class="kpi-grid">
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <span class="kpi-label">Current State</span>
            <strong>Prototype</strong>
            <span class="kpi-note">No live auth yet</span>
        </div>

        <div class="kpi-card">
            <span class="kpi-label">Production Need</span>
            <strong>RBAC</strong>
            <span class="kpi-note">Role-based access control</span>
        </div>

        <div class="kpi-card">
            <span class="kpi-label">Likely Identity</span>
            <strong>M365</strong>
            <span class="kpi-note">Microsoft sign-in / Entra ID</span>
        </div>

        <div class="kpi-card">
            <span class="kpi-label">Access Model</span>
            <strong>Scoped</strong>
            <span class="kpi-note">Role, department, and module</span>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="summary-grid user-roles-intro-grid">
        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Role Model Summary</h2>
                    <p>How permissions would work in a production version.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="user-roles-copy">
                OperationsFlow would need secure sign-in and role-based access before it could be used with live business data.
                The production model would separate users by what they are allowed to view, create, edit, approve, export,
                administer, and audit. This helps keep sensitive records controlled while still allowing owners and reviewers
                to complete follow-up work.
            </p>

            <ul class="action-list">
                <li>Admins manage users, roles, reference lists, and system settings.</li>
                <li>Managers review workload, reports, overdue items, and business pressure.</li>
                <li>Safety/compliance reviewers manage safety, risk, training, and document review records.</li>
                <li>Owners update assigned work and provide follow-up evidence.</li>
                <li>Read-only viewers can review dashboards and reports without changing records.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Prototype Boundary</h2>
                    <p>What is intentionally not built yet.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="role-boundary-highlight">
                <span>Prototype boundary</span>
                <strong>No production authentication yet</strong>
                <p>
                    The current local version keeps access simple so the workflow can be reviewed quickly.
                    A production rollout would add Microsoft identity, user accounts, permissions,
                    audit logging, access restrictions, and admin-only configuration screens.
                </p>
            </div>

            <ol class="action-list">
                <li>Add secure sign-in.</li>
                <li>Assign roles to users.</li>
                <li>Restrict sensitive pages and actions.</li>
                <li>Log important user actions.</li>
                <li>Review permissions during pilot rollout.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="role-card-grid">
        @foreach (var role in Roles)
        {
            <article class="role-card">
                <div class="role-card-header">
                    <span>@role.Number</span>
                    <div>
                        <p class="eyebrow">@role.Category</p>
                        <h2>@role.Name</h2>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p>@role.Description</p>

                <div class="role-permission-list">
                    @foreach (var permission in role.Permissions)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <strong>@permission.Title</strong>
                            <span>@permission.Description</span>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>

                <div class="role-access-note">
                    <strong>Access focus</strong>
                    <span>@role.AccessFocus</span>
                </div>
            </article>
        }
    </section>

    <section class="report-card">
        <div class="panel-header">
            <div>
                <h2>Permission Matrix</h2>
                <p>A simple production-style access model for the main workflow actions.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <table class="report-table permission-matrix-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Action</th>
                    <th>Admin</th>
                    <th>Manager</th>
                    <th>Reviewer</th>
                    <th>Owner</th>
                    <th>Viewer</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var item in PermissionMatrix)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>@item.Action</strong>
                            <span>@item.Description</span>
                        </td>
                        <td><span class="@GetPermissionBadgeClass(item.Admin)">@item.Admin</span></td>
                        <td><span class="@GetPermissionBadgeClass(item.Manager)">@item.Manager</span></td>
                        <td><span class="@GetPermissionBadgeClass(item.Reviewer)">@item.Reviewer</span></td>
                        <td><span class="@GetPermissionBadgeClass(item.Owner)">@item.Owner</span></td>
                        <td><span class="@GetPermissionBadgeClass(item.Viewer)">@item.Viewer</span></td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>

    <section class="summary-grid">
        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Access Flow</h2>
                    <p>How a production user would move through the system.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="role-flow-list">
                @foreach (var step in AccessFlow)
                {
                    <article>
                        <span>@step.Number</span>
                        <div>
                            <strong>@step.Title</strong>
                            <p>@step.Description</p>
                        </div>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Security / Audit Considerations</h2>
                    <p>Production controls that would protect the workflow.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="security-note-grid">
                @foreach (var note in SecurityNotes)
                {
                    <article>
                        <span>@note.Label</span>
                        <strong>@note.Title</strong>
                        <p>@note.Description</p>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-card">
        <div class="panel-header">
            <div>
                <h2>Page Access Map</h2>
                <p>Example role-based page visibility for a live business deployment.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <table class="report-table page-access-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Page / Module</th>
                    <th>Primary Users</th>
                    <th>Access Rule</th>
                    <th>Reason</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var item in PageAccessItems)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>@item.Page</strong></td>
                        <td><span class="@item.BadgeClass">@item.PrimaryUsers</span></td>
                        <td>@item.AccessRule</td>
                        <td>@item.Reason</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>

    <section class="summary-grid">
        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>Production Role Setup Plan</h2>
                    <p>How roles could be introduced safely.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <ol class="demo-step-list role-setup-list">
                <li>
                    <strong>Confirm user groups</strong>
                    <span>Identify admins, managers, safety/compliance reviewers, action owners, document processors, and read-only viewers.</span>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Map pages to roles</strong>
                    <span>Decide which users can open, create, edit, approve, export, and configure each module.</span>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Start with a small pilot</strong>
                    <span>Use a limited team and a limited workflow area before expanding to more users.</span>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Add audit logging</strong>
                    <span>Record important changes such as owner updates, status changes, due-date changes, completion, exports, and admin edits.</span>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Review permissions regularly</strong>
                    <span>Check access during pilot reviews so users only have what they need.</span>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="summary-panel">
            <div class="panel-header">
                <div>
                    <h2>What This Proves</h2>
                    <p>The portfolio value of this page.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="role-proof-grid">
                @foreach (var proof in ProofItems)
                {
                    <article>
                        <span>@proof.Label</span>
                        <strong>@proof.Title</strong>
                        <p>@proof.Description</p>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-card user-roles-summary-card">
        <div class="panel-header">
            <div>
                <h2>User Roles Summary</h2>
                <p>A short explanation for reviewers.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="user-roles-summary-copy">
            <p>
                OperationsFlow is currently a local workflow prototype, so it does not yet include production authentication
                or role-based permissions. A live version would need secure sign-in, assigned user roles, page-level access
                rules, action-level permissions, audit logging, and admin controls before real company data was used.
            </p>

            <p>
                The recommended production model separates administrators, managers, safety/compliance reviewers, action owners,
                document processors, standard users, and read-only viewers. This would allow the system to support real follow-up
                work while controlling who can edit records, approve changes, export reports, manage settings, and view sensitive data.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

